  "Q2: Explain how Spring manages the lifecycle of a prototype bean compared to a singleton bean, and what are the implications for resource management and thread safety?",
  "A2: A singleton bean is created once per Spring container and shared across the application, while a prototype bean is created every time it's requested. This means prototype beans aren't managed fully by the container after creation, so you have to handle cleanup. Singleton beans are thread-safe if written that way; prototype beans need special care for thread safety."
]

